==English==

===Etymology===
From {{confix|en|iso|morphism}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-us-isomorphism.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# Similarity of form
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1984|author=Brigitte Asbach-Schnitker|chapter=Introduction|title=Mercury or The Secret and Swift Messenger|isbn=9027232768
|passage=The postulated '''isomorphism''' between words and things constitutes the characterizing feature of all philosophically based universal languages.}}
## {{lb|en|biology}} the [[similarity]] in [[form]] of [[organism]]s, which may be due to [[convergent evolution]] or shared genetic background, e.g. an algae species in which the [[haploid]] and [[diploid]] life stages are indistinguishable based on morphology.
## {{lb|en|chemistry}} the similarity in the [[crystal]] structures of similar [[chemical]] [[compound]]s.
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1874|author=C. Rammelsberg|titleurl=http://books.google.co.uk/books?id=jOUEAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA197#v=onepage&q&f=true|title=Crystallographic and chemical relations of the natural sulphides, arsenides, and sulpharsenides|journal=The Chemical News and Journal of Physical Science|page=197
|passage=The '''isomorphism''' of compounds does not prove the '''isomorphism''' of their respective constituents.}}
## {{lb|en|sociology}} the similarity in the structure or [[processes]] of different [[organization]]s.
# A one-to-one correspondence
## {{lb|en|group algebra}} A [[bijection]] ''f'' such that both ''f'' and its [[inverse function|inverse]] ''f''<sup>&nbsp;&minus;1</sup> are [[homomorphism]]s, that is, structure-preserving mappings. [[Category:en:Functions]]
## {{lb|en|computer science}} a [[one-to-one]] [[correspondence]] between all the [[element]]s of two [[set]]s, e.g. the [[instance]]s of two [[class]]es, or the [[record]]s in two [[dataset]]s.
## {{lb|en|category theory}} A [[morphism]] which has a two-sided inverse; the composition of the morphism and such an inverse yields either one of two identity morphisms (depending on the order of composition).

